 {
  "id": "L18/p1",
  "lesson": "L18",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Plain data parallelism copies the whole model to every GPU and averages gradients. Does it fit?</p>",
  "answer": "c",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>Yes: eight GPUs have 640 GB between them.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>Yes, if the batch is small enough.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>No: every GPU still holds all 112 GB of model states.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>No, because gradients cannot be averaged across GPUs.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>Data parallelism splits the <strong>batch</strong>, not the model: every GPU keeps a full copy of weights, gradients and optimizer states. To use the pooled memory you must shard the model states (ZeRO), split the layers' matrices (tensor parallelism) or split the layers themselves (pipeline parallelism). The widget below adds up the memory per GPU for any combination.</p>"
 },
 {
  "id": "L18/p2",
  "lesson": "L18",
  "kind": "prediction",
  "prompt": "<p>Averaging 1 GB of gradients across 8 GPUs with a ring all-reduce, how much does each GPU send?</p>",
  "answer": "b",
  "answerKind": "choice",
  "tol": 0,
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "a",
    "html": "<p>8 GB: one copy to every other GPU.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "b",
    "html": "<p>1.75 GB.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "c",
    "html": "<p>0.125 GB: its eighth of the data.</p>"
   },
   {
    "key": "d",
    "html": "<p>1 GB.</p>"
   }
  ],
  "explain": "<p>A ring all-reduce is a reduce-scatter followed by an all-gather. Each moves <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(P-1)/P</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span> of the data through every GPU, so each sends <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mn>7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2 \\cdot 7/8 = 1.75</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">⋅</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7/8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1.75</span></span></span></span> GB, and that amount barely changes as you add GPUs. The cost of averaging gradients is set by the model size and the link bandwidth, not by the number of GPUs.</p>"
 },
 {
